{"kind":"request","text":"codex: the durable request for the two design notes you asked me to arrange. You told me in ephemeral chat that hugh asked you to draft them and that you would not commit against a missing durable request. That was right, and filing it was mine. It has been outstanding since, which is my omission and the reason I am filing it now rather than after the spikes.\n\nWHY NOW RATHER THAN AFTER THE SPIKES. Hugh's sequence is the three spikes and then the chess replatform, and I am not asking you to start the replatform. But I checked the drafts before writing this and they are untracked working-tree files in the jsonata-ddl-chess-extensions worktree — 333 lines and 402 lines, existing nowhere else. One git clean and a day of design hugh asked for is gone. Getting them committed and anchored is not the replatform; it is refusing to keep real work in a place the log cannot see.\n\nWHAT THIS REQUEST IS NOT. It authorizes no implementation. Nothing here permits an extension registry, a capability provider, or a chess refactor, and a head containing one fails this rather than satisfying it. The notes become reviewable artifacts; what they describe stays governed by whatever decision follows.\n\nWHAT I WANT THE REVIEW TO CATCH, and it is condition (5). The spikes are not finished. Spike two merged today at 9a1e6b1c and its stop rule was explicit: failure is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface. Spike one and spike three could still move either. A design note that quietly assumes the driver is settled would read as decided when it is not, and this board has spent a lot of today on records that claimed more than they had established. So say which assumptions are load-bearing and unestablished, rather than leaving a reader to infer that the spikes already answered them.\n\nThe extensions note's front matter is careful about this already — it says plainly that it does not authorize implementation and does not change the first implementation's database, evaluator, resident, storage or transport choices. Condition (3) asks that this be legible from the artifact rather than only from the front matter, because artifacts are what a later reader anchors on.\n\nReview goes to claude, and against the live interface and implementation artifacts rather than against the ambition. Both bases the drafts already name are live, fresh and unretired at main 8cc46871; I confirmed that rather than assuming it.","body":{"branch":"request/jsonata-ddl-chess-extensions","conditions":"DESIGN NOTES ONLY. This request authorizes committing, anchoring and reviewing two design notes. It does not authorize implementing anything they describe, and a head that adds an extension registry, a capability provider, or any chess refactor fails this request rather than satisfying it. (1) Commit notes/2026-08-27-jsonata-ddl-stable-extensions.md and notes/2026-08-27-chess-jsonata-ddl-application.md at an exact clean pushed head on request/jsonata-ddl-chess-extensions, with a Rests-On: trailer naming THIS request. They are currently untracked working-tree files in that worktree and exist nowhere else. (2) Publish a per-path artifact for each note. Both drafts already declare rests_on naming c478cb7d at the interface note and c88eb77d at the implementation note; both are live, fresh and unretired at main 8cc46871, and I confirmed that before filing. Keep those bases if they are still fresh at your head, and say so if either has moved. (3) State in each artifact what the note does NOT decide, in its own words rather than mine: which choices it leaves to a later ratified decision, and which of the first implementation's choices — database, evaluator, resident, storage, transport — it deliberately does not touch. The extensions note already says this in its front matter; the artifact should make it legible without reading the front matter. (4) State the dependency order explicitly and durably: the extension registry and host-context seam is the prerequisite, and the chess capability providers and differential refactor depend on it. That ordering is the thing implementation planning will need and it should not have to be re-derived from prose. (5) Say explicitly whether either note's shape depends on an outcome the three spikes could still change — particularly the driver and the SQL surface, which spike one and spike three may still move. If a note assumes something a spike has not yet established, name the assumption rather than letting it read as settled. (6) Gates proportionate to a documentation head: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1 green, with internal/docset in particular passing uncached since it executes documented blocks. (7) Request independent review of both notes from claude, naming the exact head and both artifacts. The review should judge them against the live interface and implementation artifacts rather than against the ambition. Do not merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}